 ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ${e}^{-t}$ |  |  | $9$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-6{e}^{2t})-5{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{2t} & -5{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{2t} & -5{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}12{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{t} \\\\ 9{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-337281/field-1/b3446ac7d1cab066ed54dfe96ad264fd252e20c09a1eb0d7677170e385e452df" :answer/id #uuid "a1d22408-9a53-5312-be19-f8235d22d2c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{22}{5}e^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337281/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "00889b2e-d5d0-5138-b077-912960e6b3d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337281/field-1/b3446ac7d1cab066ed54dfe96ad264fd252e20c09a1eb0d7677170e385e452df" :answer-field/choices ["q-337281/field-1/b3446ac7d1cab066ed54dfe96ad264fd252e20c09a1eb0d7677170e385e452df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337281/field-2/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "0c0555de-f0d7-5158-a983-8a53fd561939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337281/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "18c3e95b-741a-5126-95b1-224f69f73413" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337281/field-2/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer-field/choices ["q-337281/field-2/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337281" :question/id #uuid "2857f53c-ce32-5579-ae3e-edbb18aabeb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337281" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}$ |  |  | $-5$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-4{e}^{-3t})-5{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-3t} & -5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-3t} & -5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} & -5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} & -5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{t} \\\\ -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-337281/field-1" "q-337281/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337281"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336699/field-1/4ab5c0ba8310b59e3ee77ebcf7d0b9a55eb5706843fbf5e5d62dbccc2cf5c81d" :answer/id #uuid "38b6294c-18c9-5bd6-b5fe-6c318a036071" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3e^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336699/field-1/4d5c3bbb162444faa8cfa81f6390a0717b79a525a5db0dbdf9e68a8b556de557" :answer/id #uuid "db617487-9c49-56d9-a93b-47fc7444f682" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3e^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336699/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "17d79a92-2748-55e0-a38e-35a1b989a036" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336699/field-1/4ab5c0ba8310b59e3ee77ebcf7d0b9a55eb5706843fbf5e5d62dbccc2cf5c81d" :answer-field/choices ["q-336699/field-1/4ab5c0ba8310b59e3ee77ebcf7d0b9a55eb5706843fbf5e5d62dbccc2cf5c81d" "q-336699/field-1/4d5c3bbb162444faa8cfa81f6390a0717b79a525a5db0dbdf9e68a8b556de557"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336699/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "d4dfcfa5-1d69-5378-b3ff-281b6dd6eb78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336699/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4973c3ce-d70b-5651-8464-19f378e73dc7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336699/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-336699/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336699" :question/id #uuid "5410bedb-33cd-5e6c-885a-74e25e6e1037" :question/math-academy-id "q-336699" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $5{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-({e}^{3t})\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -3{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-336699/field-1" "q-336699/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336699"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337280/field-1/8c16dd4a8c2e42d197aaee4a981203b04d86bea496b8d5878a8cc2a010371347" :answer/id #uuid "02933199-7613-5d8e-8527-93b5f5e310a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5e^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337280/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "438186cf-e28f-55e2-bc0a-165746ccd322" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337280/field-1/8c16dd4a8c2e42d197aaee4a981203b04d86bea496b8d5878a8cc2a010371347" :answer-field/choices ["q-337280/field-1/8c16dd4a8c2e42d197aaee4a981203b04d86bea496b8d5878a8cc2a010371347"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337280/field-2/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "aa8feda5-bd99-5f75-a416-17048daba91b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337280/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce28f38f-e98f-5240-8eab-696979a64884" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337280/field-2/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer-field/choices ["q-337280/field-2/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337280" :question/id #uuid "f3c1ca24-1032-5ae4-8b6d-0320c87ffa14" :question/math-academy-id "q-337280" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}$ |  |  | $\\frac{17}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-3{e}^{4t})-3{e}^{4t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}}[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-337280/field-1" "q-337280/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337280"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-329817/field-1/bda7d9c5da4b84e0a8c59d672c878965927a1b3c3b8aecb25a21c25ca8ab531a" :answer/id #uuid "0d427c70-395b-59f9-8a4e-a0096c7d539a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-18t-15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164041 :answer-field/correct "q-329817/field-1/bda7d9c5da4b84e0a8c59d672c878965927a1b3c3b8aecb25a21c25ca8ab531a" :answer-field/choices ["q-329817/field-1/bda7d9c5da4b84e0a8c59d672c878965927a1b3c3b8aecb25a21c25ca8ab531a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-329817"] :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}12t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-12t-6$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}12t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 8t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-4t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 8t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 8t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}+\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=8t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=8\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =8t\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t{e}^{-t}+\\int 8\\,{e}^{-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t{e}^{-t}-8{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-t}(1+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ -8{e}^{-t}(1+t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t)-16(1+t) \\\\ 2(1-2t)-8(1+t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18t-15 \\\\ -12t-6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-337307/field-1/029c86843ba4120bb07bca13d9013e86072d06679a2e1c058fe894d5443ef1f7" :answer/id #uuid "89813649-c7dd-5dba-84e7-96ba01dffd96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12t+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337307/field-1/91194b77215911dc13a0dad1df82e1e5e98a73899192ae17c06e921c59121ca6" :answer/id #uuid "860d81ff-7e8e-5210-ac25-9b816e957be3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12t-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337307/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "97d2da6a-930b-55ad-878b-2512bc59f88b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337307/field-1/029c86843ba4120bb07bca13d9013e86072d06679a2e1c058fe894d5443ef1f7" :answer-field/choices ["q-337307/field-1/029c86843ba4120bb07bca13d9013e86072d06679a2e1c058fe894d5443ef1f7" "q-337307/field-1/91194b77215911dc13a0dad1df82e1e5e98a73899192ae17c06e921c59121ca6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337307" :question/id #uuid "69e79418-c556-5772-9366-f70f716cc80e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337307" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | $30t+20$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{6}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{1}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}5(3t-1)+45(t+1) \\\\ 5(3t-1)+9(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}30t+20 \\\\ 12t+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337307/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337307"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337288/field-1/27c2f746d92515301e43ea96d9b962ae6e5e63097af3fb43be9c1c1294d0304f" :answer/id #uuid "3487218e-1a03-5769-a408-ca276a27d3b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4t-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337288/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9bee8842-2972-59b0-a604-8fb394719907" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337288/field-1/27c2f746d92515301e43ea96d9b962ae6e5e63097af3fb43be9c1c1294d0304f" :answer-field/choices ["q-337288/field-1/27c2f746d92515301e43ea96d9b962ae6e5e63097af3fb43be9c1c1294d0304f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337288" :question/id #uuid "6c9d5a60-2dd6-52cb-b5d2-1016201cd266" :question/math-academy-id "q-337288" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $6t-6$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-2t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-2\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ 3{e}^{t}(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t)+6(t-1) \\\\ 6(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4t-5 \\\\ 6t-6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337288/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337288"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337286/field-1/70c168fc7330c3014c36f3e76d8a4ea65da165ad41297dbc7c1e414f3f771ab0" :answer/id #uuid "1058c831-1405-5fa1-a083-44f30c41b355" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3t+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337286/field-1/6bc8fad155fc33d1b3914eab0594ee26bd7a60dc9e9de1405f15034dbf28b380" :answer/id #uuid "cf0708fe-bd9c-5455-a9f9-7b4135fafe3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3t-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337286/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3414a190-78a6-540d-bedd-6f1ed341aae0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337286/field-1/70c168fc7330c3014c36f3e76d8a4ea65da165ad41297dbc7c1e414f3f771ab0" :answer-field/choices ["q-337286/field-1/70c168fc7330c3014c36f3e76d8a4ea65da165ad41297dbc7c1e414f3f771ab0" "q-337286/field-1/6bc8fad155fc33d1b3914eab0594ee26bd7a60dc9e9de1405f15034dbf28b380"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337286" :question/id #uuid "af61cfa8-d3e0-5ba1-9907-4c274c821745" :question/math-academy-id "q-337286" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $23-15t$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot 3{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-4t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}(-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{t}+\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{t}(1-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4(3t-1)+9(1-t) \\\\ 4(3t-1)+27(1-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3t+5 \\\\ 23-15t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337286/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337286"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337306/field-1/ced371a64215106847a8a35c25a49e35cffc7143e767af726cf52cdc68fff7ec" :answer/id #uuid "fe455121-f052-5722-a275-63c873463d8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21t+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337306/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8967ffe-ea15-59de-bc9f-24c3596e24ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337306/field-1/ced371a64215106847a8a35c25a49e35cffc7143e767af726cf52cdc68fff7ec" :answer-field/choices ["q-337306/field-1/ced371a64215106847a8a35c25a49e35cffc7143e767af726cf52cdc68fff7ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337306" :question/id #uuid "435c6319-4985-51ca-87c3-285497ad7b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337306" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $39t+23$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot 3{e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\cdot (-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}-\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4(3t-1)+9(t+1) \\\\ 4(3t-1)+27(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}21t+5 \\\\ 39t+23\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337306/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337306"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21546"] :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) =\nA\n\\mathbf{x}(t) +\n\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t),$ where $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for $\\mathbf x_p(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}'_p(t) = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) + \\Phi' \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) + \\Phi' \\mathbf{u}(t) = A \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi' = A \\Phi$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{u}'(t) = \\Phi^{-1}(t) \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L8}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the fact that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/81e146c6f6297f8f30340d63ddaec02efe3d6442005f5852a70d5be22ceed764.png) matrix.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/09febb48bb00d22caebf088103181b5db6377befccad18f1370718351cc91e9d.png) both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\textrm{L7}$ into ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/9be3bba9e4f49ae67d635fe7b6260607d3e776f03baeaf8d5a10d650e0b0b987.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L4}.$ Since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) =A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $\\mathbf{c}(t)$ of $\\Phi$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}'(t) =A\\mathbf{c}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}'(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $\\Phi.$ As a result, this means that $\\Phi' = A \\Phi.$ Therefore, $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the fact that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/f3cf7b9339acd3d9b370c67a182509bff354728494afda00e2e991f3a09996af.png) matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}.$ Since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $\\Phi$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/8ba4d14f89d4c90c90f84e0618b0cf8dedadbf2c3989cc9533e44590100357d1.png) both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1},$ $\\textrm{L7},$ and $\\textrm{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\textrm{L7}$ into $\\textrm{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\textrm{L7}$ into  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/4d1fa76e9619981c1b36c457c5247dad023bdbb7f7810fdc9e5b9105028c4e22.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21547"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/2b22742201ba6932de40a628419faef0405bc8ae0ad26814bc7ca515455e3e5c.png)\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute $\\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t)$ and the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ of the system." :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/0c7accec33fc7c9d6b2a228c8ec4bcc454f687be7f96ab615b3b818f82fb8cc8.png)\n\nSince $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-2{e}^{-4t})-2{e}^{-4t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-4t} & -2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-4t} & -2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{3t} & -2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{t} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21548"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/bc92d310e939c8688ab770a51107d1218d5b8878a877d7b3a64af770d002a9ca.png)\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/860251c7cc4c1259c4660b41aae1f3e0b0177e13c9372dd941422040f0f7e5ee.png) |  | $\\boxed{\\rule{0pt}{1.3em}\\phantom{xxxxxxxx}}$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/e8db6a28a4671312259595de0609cefb8db0d5fc441e122885335e1165e0cd22.png)\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/742a0ea79220f0dc0055918b611393d69518bc5c73ef9b41b6fd068c8c75ad4c.png)\n\nSince $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}-0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 0\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=2t$ and $\\text{d}v=e^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}-\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, the integrand is zero, so the integral is zero.\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
